\frametitle{Five mistakes to avoid}
\protect\phantomsection\label{five-mistakes-to-avoid}
\begin{enumerate}
\tightlist
\item
  Forgetting the square root (or the \(\sqrt n\)) when computing the
  standard error.
\item
  Using \(z_{1-\alpha}\) (one-sided) for a two-sided interval: 95\%
  \(\to\) \(1.96\), not \(1.645\).
\item
  Using the normal instead of \(t_{n-1}\) for small samples with
  estimated variance.
\item
  Mixing up Type I (reject a true \(H_0\)) and Type II (fail to reject a
  false \(H_0\)).
\item
  Reporting the margin of error when the question asks for the width
  (\(2\times\) margin).
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{More practice:} the 10-question multiple-choice set in
\emph{materials for TA} (\texttt{ci\_ht\_questions1.pdf}) uses exactly
these skills.
